  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We tackle the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem using a convex optimization hierarchy (i.e., scenario-based approach) that leverages the fact that the maximum overlap among all shifts can be expressed as the maximum eigenvalue of a certain covariance matrix. This transforms the non-convex optimization into a sequence of convex problems. We start from the given 47-point initial solution and refine it by:\n\n   - Interpreting the problem as minimizing the maximum \"autocorrelation\" score computed over all shifts\n   - Formulating this minimax problem by introducing auxiliary variables and constraints\n   - Projecting the initial solution to meet the sum constraint and intensity bounds\n   - Iteratively solving a convex problem using an alternate projection-gradient method for enhanced convergence\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Variables:\n     - `h \u2208 \u211d^{n_points}` with `0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1` for all `i`\n     - `\u03b2`: A set of auxiliary variables representing the overlap scores for different shifts\n     - `\u03b3`: A variable representing the maximum overlap\n\n   - Objective:\n     - Minimize `\u03b3` (upper bound for every shift's overlap)\n\n   - Constraints:\n     - Primary: `0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1` for all `i`\n     - Secondary: `1 = (dx * sum(h[i]))` (volume constraint)\n     - Overlap representation:\n        `\u03b2[k] = (dx\u00b2 * sum{h[i] * (1-h[i+k])})` for each `k`\n        `\u03b3 \u2265 \u03b2[k]` for all `k`\n        `\u03b2[k] \u2264 \u03b3` for all `k`\n     - Express \u03b2[k] using discrete correlation:\n        `\u03b2[k] = dx * (smoothed correlation at shift k)`\n\n   - Convexification:\n     - Re-express \u03b2[k] using discrete correlation coefficients\n     - Formulate the minimax problem as:\n        `min \u03b3 s.t. \u03b3 \u2265 (dx * max_{k} correlation[k])`\n     - Convert into a convex problem by considering a relaxation where \u03c9\u2096 is a slack variable:\n\n        `\u03c9\u2096 \u2265 correlation[k]`\n        `\u03b3 \u2265 (dx * \u03c9\u2096)`\n\n   - Equivalence preservation:\n     - Ensures the original objective is matched for any `h` satisfying the constraints\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Given `initial_h_values`, first ensure `sum(initial_h_values.*) = n_points/2` and all values in [0,1]\n   - Initialize `\u03b3\u2080` to the current score and `n_points = len(initial_h_values)`\n   - Compute the initial score using `np.correlate` and scale by `dx`\n   - Construct the score vector:\n        `shift_scores = [np.correlate(h_full, 1-h_full, mode='full')]` (scaled appropriately)\n\n   - Optimization loop (max 1000 iterations or time budget):\n     - Build `A` matrix (linear relations for overlap representation)\n     - Solve the convex program using CVXPY with the problem:\n\n        minimize \u03b3\u2080\n        subject to\n           0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2200i\n           sum(h) = n_points/2\n           \u03b2\u2096 \u2264 \u03b3\u2080 for all shifts\n\n     - If feasible solution exists, compute its score using the built-in evaluator\n\n   - Use projected gradient descent to handle constraints for 50% of iterations with active learning  \n   - If time runs low (below 100 seconds remaining), perform a simpler forward pass on a small neighborhood around the best candidate\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Initial optimization uses `n_points=47` (given initial solution)\n   - If no significant improvement, attempt a resolution doubling (n=94, dx halved) to refi\n.   - Conservative setting: learning rate \u03b1 = 0.05 for gradient descent steps\n   - Active learning triggers after achieving 5 consecutive iterations without improvement\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Main computation: O(n_points\u00b3) per CVXPY solve (for small n)\n   - Conclusion: relaxation to approximately O(1) per iteration with n=47\n   - Project 100 iterations at 5-20 seconds per iteration\n   - Time: 500 seconds consumed, remaining evaluated by fallback\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Ensure all candidates satisfy `0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1` and `sum(h)*dx=1`\n   - Compute final score using provided correlation method\n   - Must not exceed time budget\n\n   Checks:\n   - h[i] in [0,1] for all i\n   - sum1 = sum(h)*dx == 1 (numerically)\n   - score = max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * dx",
